1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,720
 Now, one last touch before we get started with this course.

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:06,560
 I will be referring to the Share Responsibility model quite a lot,

3
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:09,200
 and you will see this diagram at the end of this course.

4
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:14,640
 This is what defines what is your responsibility versus AWS when using the cloud,

5
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:16,400
 and there is a Share Responsibility.

6
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:21,120
 You, as a customer, you're responsible for the security in the cloud.

7
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:24,400
 So, whatever you use in the cloud, however you configure it,

8
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:26,400
 is your entire responsibility.

9
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:29,520
 That includes security, your data, your operating system,

10
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:32,560
 your network and firewall configuration, etc, etc.

11
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:37,120
 And AWS is going to be responsible for the security of the cloud.

12
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:39,840
 So, all the infrastructure, all the hardware,

13
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:44,720
 or the software, or their own internal security, they are responsible of.

14
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:47,360
 And this is why we have Share Responsibility.

15
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:49,200
 In the Certified Cloud Petitioner exam,

16
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:51,520
 you will be asked some questions around finding

17
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:55,360
 what is your responsibility and what is the responsibility of AWS.

18
00:00:55,360 --> 00:00:57,920
 So, I just wanted to give you a quick overview of this,

19
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:01,840
 and in some sections, I will be referring back to this diagram and this model

20
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:06,480
 to tell you what is your responsibility and what is the responsibility of AWS.

21
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:12,240
 Finally, when you use AWS, you are agreeing to their acceptable use policy,

22
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:14,880
 which you can find right here, and I think it's pretty obvious,

23
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:19,040
 but you cannot do any illegal, harmful, or offensive use or content.

24
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:23,120
 You cannot do any security violation, you cannot abuse a network,

25
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:26,640
 and you cannot do email or other types of messages of use.

26
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:29,440
 All of that makes sense, but it's good to specify it.

27
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:32,320
 Now, I hope you're excited, we're going to get started with this course,

28
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:36,400
 and actually get to use the cloud, so I will see you in the next section.

